# KNOWN GAP (R47 Track F5a) — `parent_dir` in the self-host loader materializes
# ONE heap String PER CHARACTER of the path it is given.
#
# THE SHAPE, copied from `tests/fixtures/self_host_lowerer/loader.gg:26-38`:
# a `while` loop over the path's indices whose body does
# `String ch = path.slice(i, i + 1)` just to compare one character to "/".
# That bind is a `VarDeclFromBorrow` on a fresh 1-character String, so the loop
# costs `len(path)` calls to `gorget_string_clone_to_owned` — for an operation
# whose whole job is to find the last '/'.
#
# WHY IT IS MORE THAN AN INEFFICIENCY. `parent_dir` is called 7 times per
# self-host compile, on paths rooted at the PROJECT ROOT, so the compiler's
# total `string_clone` count is a linear function of how long the absolute path
# to your checkout happens to be:
#
#   string_clone = K + 7 x len(repo-root path)
#
# measured on ONE binary at four root paths spanning 6..84 characters, zero
# residual, and attributed to this ONE CloneId (site #2985, `loader.gg:32:25`,
# `VarDeclFromBorrow`, `String`) which accounted for the whole delta. That is
# what made the R47 clone ratchet read 31,474,212 in `/workspace/gorget` and
# 31,474,506 in an agent worktree 42 characters deeper: 7 x 42 = 294.
#
# INTENDED BEHAVIOUR: finding the last '/' in a path allocates a number of
# Strings that does NOT scale with the path's length. The `#[ignore]`d test
# `known_gap_sh_parent_dir_clones_one_string_per_path_char` asserts exactly
# that, by running this program over a short and a long path and requiring the
# two `string_clone` totals to be EQUAL. Today they differ by the length delta.
#
# ⚠ KEEP `.slice()` HERE — BUT NOT FOR THE REASON THIS COMMENT USED TO GIVE.
#
# It used to say the fixture was allowlisted in
# `tests/lints.rs::no_dot_slice_after_d22`, and that the colon form `s[a:b]`
# "materializes NOTHING — measured 1,002 vs 2". Both halves are dead:
#
#   * THE LINT IS GONE. `no_dot_slice_after_d22` and its allowlist were
#     retired (owner ruling 2026-09-03) — see the note where it stood.
#     `.slice()` and `.substring()` are permanent aliases under D22 Rider 2,
#     so nothing bans the spelling and nothing needs to exempt this file.
#   * THE MEASUREMENT IS INVERTED. `s[a:b]` did not materialize because it
#     handed out a dangling cap=0 VIEW — the `t0871` use-after-free, fixed
#     2026-09-03. The two spellings now cost exactly the same. Transcribing
#     THIS loop to the colon form and running both:
#         path len  4 → string_clone=7  BOTH spellings
#         path len 64 → string_clone=67 BOTH spellings
#     Regenerate with `gg build --clones=stats` on the two transcriptions;
#     `slice_spelling_cost_equivalence` in `tests/integration.rs` is the
#     standing guard that they stay equal.
#
# So the reason to keep `.slice()` is now simply that it is the spelling the
# self-host actually uses at this site. What the fixture holds is UNCHANGED
# and is the real point: the clone count SCALES WITH THE PATH LENGTH (7 → 67
# for 4 → 64 chars), one heap String per character scanned. Switching
# spellings does not fix that, and never would have. Rewriting to `split`
# WOULD make it green and hide the thing it exists to hold.

from std.os import args

String parent_dir(String path):
    # Extract parent directory: find last '/' by scanning
    int last_slash = -1
    int i = 0
    while i < path.len():
        if i + 1 <= path.len():
            String ch = path.slice(i, i + 1)
            if ch == "/":
                last_slash = i
        i += 1
    if last_slash >= 0:
        return path.slice(0, last_slash)
    return "."

void main():
    # The path comes from argv so ONE binary can be measured at two input
    # lengths — the clone COUNT is the observable here, not stdout.
    Vector[String] argv = args()
    String p = "/a/b"
    if argv.len() > 1:
        p = argv.get(1).unwrap()
    print(parent_dir(p))
